installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained AlmaLinux 9.6 with kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6 after installation and restart. The installed AlmaLinux EL9 MariaDB packages ran successfully and recovered after reboot.","evidence_ids":["global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","cmd-43ffec6d64474d7c","cmd-7314456f4eb74eb5","cmd-003850c105ac41b7","cmd-1cddcc9b4d054cb4","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Core mutations—package installation, database configuration/import, accounts, restricted firewall access, client configuration, transactional tests, and required restarts—had clear task or evidence roles. Two kinds of unnecessary retained residue remain. First, cmd-7b21db6c937e4d88 wrote the cluster private SSH key to node1 for a one-time credential transfer; no later removal is captured, so the sensitive key is treated as retained. That command also prepared node1 inbound authorization despite no demonstrated need, although the evidence cannot establish whether its conditional key append occurred. cmd-20e516efdb3d4656 prepared node2 authorization for the transfer, but whether it added a new persistent key is likewise unobserved. Second, cmd-51d77f8797ac47aa and cmd-c545577328a84dd6 left /tmp/benchmark-reboot.log on node1 and node2; lifecycle snapshots directly show both low-risk files remained. No failed services or package inconsistency were observed after execution.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7b21db6c937e4d88","cmd-20e516efdb3d4656","cmd-180aa64fa10b4fd7","cmd-51d77f8797ac47aa","cmd-c545577328a84dd6","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: healthy pre-restart observations, acknowledgements, changed boot IDs independently corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, data, permissions, platform, and remote reporting behavior. Hygiene is reduced by retained reboot logs and an apparently retained cluster private SSH key used for the one-time credential transfer.","limitations":["Final lifecycle collection does not inventory /root, so retention of node1's written SSH private key is inferred from the complete timeline showing its creation and no cleanup; whether either conditional authorized_keys append actually changed state is indeterminate.","Lifecycle package-health observations report a successful DNF consistency check but provide no detailed package listing; runtime and reboot evidence nevertheless directly demonstrate MariaDB compatibility."]}
